    "statement0": "<p style=\"margin-bottom: 12px;\">The word <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"inactive\" </i></span> is mentioned in <span style=\"color: rgba(255, 89, 98, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">0.2%</span> of papers' Results sections, as of 2020-2024. This puts in the bottom <span style=\"color: rgba(237, 12, 24, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">46<sup>th</sup></span> percentile of words.</p>Among papers containing <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"inactive\" </i></span>, <span style=\"color: rgba(94, 184, 242, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">31.4%</span> of reported significant p-values (p < .05) are 'fragile' (.01 \u2264 p < .05) - i.e., just barely fall under the standard significance threshold. This is a higher rate than the median Psychology paper (median shown in black); relative to other words, papers with <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"inactive\" </i></span> are in the top <span style=\"color: rgba(3, 152, 252, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">76<sup>th</sup></span> percentile of fragile p-value percentages. Note, strong/weak p-values highly predict replicability.",
    "statement1": "As of 2020-2024, papers containing <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"inactive\" </i></span> tend to be published in higher impact factor journals (<span style=\"color: rgba(105, 219, 99, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">68<sup>th</sup></span> percentile among all words)  and tend to be cited more (<span style=\"color: rgba(255, 168, 82, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">88<sup>th</sup></span> percentile word). In general, this word is negatively associated with university ranking, such that lower-ranked institutions are more likely to publish papers containing <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"inactive\" </i></span> (<span style=\"color: rgba(170, 117, 250, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">42<sup>nd</sup></span> percentile). "
